WEBVTT

00:00.650 --> 00:03.390
You may think that the
mean value theorem is just

00:03.390 --> 00:07.180
this arcane theorem that
shows up in calculus classes.

00:07.180 --> 00:09.100
But what we will
see in this video

00:09.100 --> 00:11.520
is that it has actually been
used-- at least implicitly

00:11.520 --> 00:14.930
used-- to give people
speeding tickets.

00:14.930 --> 00:16.309
So let's think of an example.

00:16.309 --> 00:19.070
So let's say that this is
a toll booth, right here.

00:19.070 --> 00:23.190
You're on the turnpike, and
this is a toll booth at point A.

00:23.190 --> 00:28.120
And you get your toll-- you
reach it at exactly 1:00 PM,

00:28.120 --> 00:31.040
and then the highway's computers
and stuff register that.

00:31.040 --> 00:34.279
Let's say you have some type
of-- one of those devices

00:34.279 --> 00:36.570
so that when you pay the toll
it just knows who you are

00:36.570 --> 00:39.050
and it registers-- it takes
your money from an account

00:39.050 --> 00:39.890
someplace.

00:39.890 --> 00:46.120
So it sees that you got
there at exactly 1:00 PM.

00:46.120 --> 00:50.460
And then, let's say
that you get off

00:50.460 --> 00:53.770
of the toll highway,
the turnpike.

00:53.770 --> 00:55.990
Let's say you get
off of it at point B,

00:55.990 --> 00:58.040
and you get there
exactly 2:00 PM.

00:58.040 --> 01:01.290
I'm making these numbers
very easy to work with.

01:01.290 --> 01:03.810
And let's say that they
are 80 miles apart.

01:03.810 --> 01:06.255
So this distance right
over here is 80 miles.

01:09.524 --> 01:10.940
And let's say that
the speed limit

01:10.940 --> 01:14.100
on this stretch of highway
is 55 miles per hour.

01:21.930 --> 01:25.090
So the question is,
can the authorities

01:25.090 --> 01:30.070
prove that you went
over the speed limit?

01:30.070 --> 01:31.510
Well, let's just graph this.

01:31.510 --> 01:33.780
I think you know
where this is going.

01:33.780 --> 01:35.040
So let's graph it.

01:35.040 --> 01:38.630
So let's say this right
over here is our position.

01:38.630 --> 01:42.670
So I'll call that the
s-axis, s for position.

01:42.670 --> 01:45.480
And that's going to be in miles.

01:45.480 --> 01:49.280
And s is, obviously, s doesn't
really stand for position.

01:49.280 --> 01:52.360
But p, you know, it kind of
looks like rho for density.

01:52.360 --> 01:55.840
And d we use for differentials
for distance or displacement.

01:55.840 --> 02:01.440
So s is what gets used
for position very often.

02:01.440 --> 02:03.670
So let's say s is our position.

02:03.670 --> 02:05.740
And let's see,
this is t for time.

02:09.030 --> 02:12.110
And let's say this is in hours.

02:12.110 --> 02:14.040
And let's see, we care
about the interval

02:14.040 --> 02:22.560
from time going from
time 1 to time 2.

02:22.560 --> 02:25.730
I'm not really drawing the
axes completely at scale.

02:25.730 --> 02:28.160
Would you let me just assume
that there's a gap here

02:28.160 --> 02:32.190
just because I
don't actually want

02:32.190 --> 02:37.301
to make you think that I'm
drawing it completely at scale.

02:37.301 --> 02:39.800
Because I really want to focus
on this part of the interval.

02:39.800 --> 02:43.210
So this is time
equals to 2 hours.

02:43.210 --> 02:48.375
And so at time equal 1,
you're right over here.

02:48.375 --> 02:53.670
And let's say this position is,
we'll just call that s of 1.

02:53.670 --> 02:59.885
And at time 2, you're at this
position right over here.

02:59.885 --> 03:02.200
You're right over there.

03:02.200 --> 03:06.860
And so your position is s of 2.

03:06.860 --> 03:09.460
You're at that coordinate
right over there.

03:09.460 --> 03:11.739
And that's all we know.

03:11.739 --> 03:12.530
That's all we know.

03:12.530 --> 03:13.905
Well, we know a
few other things.

03:13.905 --> 03:17.310
We know what our change in
time is, it's 2 minus 1.

03:17.310 --> 03:19.570
And we know what our
change in position is.

03:19.570 --> 03:24.760
We know that our
change in position,

03:24.760 --> 03:35.000
which is equal to s of 2 minus
s of 1, is equal to 80 miles.

03:35.000 --> 03:37.725
The change in
position is 80 miles.

03:37.725 --> 03:39.850
So let me write that, and
we'll just for simplicity

03:39.850 --> 03:41.224
assume it was a
straight highway.

03:41.224 --> 03:43.640
So our change in distance
is the same as our change

03:43.640 --> 03:46.260
in position, same as
change in displacement.

03:46.260 --> 03:47.905
So this is 80 miles.

03:51.350 --> 03:54.000
And then what is
our change in time?

03:54.000 --> 03:58.825
Over our change in time, well
that's going to be 2 minus 1.

04:03.130 --> 04:05.115
Which is just
going to be 1 hour.

04:08.340 --> 04:12.000
Or we could say that the
slope of the line that

04:12.000 --> 04:13.650
connects these two
points-- let me

04:13.650 --> 04:17.670
do that in another color--
that's the same color--

04:17.670 --> 04:24.180
the slope of this line right
over here is 80 miles per hour.

04:24.180 --> 04:30.900
Slope is equal to
80 miles per hour.

04:30.900 --> 04:35.810
Or you could say that your
average velocity over that hour

04:35.810 --> 04:37.710
was 80 miles per hour.

04:37.710 --> 04:40.180
And what the authorities
could do in a court of law,

04:40.180 --> 04:42.270
and I've never heard
a mathematical theorem

04:42.270 --> 04:43.620
cited like this, but they could.

04:43.620 --> 04:46.170
And I remember reading about
this about 10 years ago,

04:46.170 --> 04:47.540
and it was very controversial.

04:47.540 --> 04:50.000
The authorities said
look, over this interval,

04:50.000 --> 04:53.360
your average velocity was
clearly 80 miles per hour.

04:53.360 --> 04:56.760
So at some point in that hour--
and they could have cited,

04:56.760 --> 04:59.500
they could have said by
the mean value theorem--

04:59.500 --> 05:02.120
at some point in
that hour, you must

05:02.120 --> 05:05.210
have been going at exactly
80 miles, at least,

05:05.210 --> 05:07.887
frankly, 80 miles per hour.

05:07.887 --> 05:09.720
And it would have been
very hard to disprove

05:09.720 --> 05:13.200
because your position
as a function of time

05:13.200 --> 05:15.640
is definitely continuous
and differentiable

05:15.640 --> 05:16.890
over that interval.

05:16.890 --> 05:18.920
It's continuous,
you're not just getting

05:18.920 --> 05:20.630
teleported from one
place to another.

05:20.630 --> 05:22.520
That would be a
pretty amazing car.

05:22.520 --> 05:23.770
And it is also differentiable.

05:23.770 --> 05:26.500
You always have a
well defined velocity.

05:26.500 --> 05:28.790
And so I challenge anyone.

05:28.790 --> 05:30.550
Try to connect these
two points with

05:30.550 --> 05:33.530
a continuous and differentiable
curve, where at some point

05:33.530 --> 05:37.080
the instantaneous velocity,
the slope of the tangent line,

05:37.080 --> 05:40.050
is not the same thing as
the slope of this line.

05:40.050 --> 05:41.370
It's impossible.

05:41.370 --> 05:43.730
The mean value theorem
tells us it's impossible.

05:43.730 --> 05:45.030
So let me just draw.

05:45.030 --> 05:46.050
So we could imagine.

05:46.050 --> 05:50.670
Say I had to stop to
pay, to kind of register

05:50.670 --> 05:52.880
where I am on the
highway, then I

05:52.880 --> 05:54.460
start to accelerate
a little bit.

05:54.460 --> 05:57.452
So right now, my
instantaneous velocity

05:57.452 --> 05:59.510
is less than my
average velocity.

05:59.510 --> 06:00.840
I'm accelerating.

06:00.840 --> 06:02.550
The slope of the tangent line.

06:02.550 --> 06:05.710
But if I want to get
there at that time,

06:05.710 --> 06:08.310
and especially because I have
to slow down as I approach it,

06:08.310 --> 06:09.904
as I approach the tollbooth.

06:09.904 --> 06:12.320
The only way I could connect
these two things-- well let's

06:12.320 --> 06:14.695
see, I'm going to have to--
at some point, at this point,

06:14.695 --> 06:17.360
I'm actually going faster
than the 80 miles per hour.

06:17.360 --> 06:19.710
And the mean value theorem
just tells us, that look,

06:19.710 --> 06:22.130
that this function is
continuous and differentiable

06:22.130 --> 06:23.010
over this interval.

06:23.010 --> 06:24.850
Continuous over the
closed interval.

06:24.850 --> 06:26.580
Differentiable over
the open interval.

06:26.580 --> 06:29.420
That there's at least one point
in the open interval, which

06:29.420 --> 06:33.070
it calls c, so there's
at least one point where

06:33.070 --> 06:34.810
your instantaneous
rate of change, where

06:34.810 --> 06:36.550
the slope of the
tangent line, is

06:36.550 --> 06:39.260
the same as the slope
as the secant line.

06:39.260 --> 06:41.726
So that point right
over there, that point

06:41.726 --> 06:43.100
looks like that
right over there.

06:43.100 --> 06:45.760
And so if this is time c,
that looks like it's like

06:45.760 --> 06:49.370
at around 1:15, this-- the
mean value theorem says

06:49.370 --> 06:54.610
that at some point, there
exists some time where

06:54.610 --> 06:59.920
s prime of c is equal to
this average velocity,

06:59.920 --> 07:02.356
is equal to 80 miles per hour.

07:02.356 --> 07:04.230
And it doesn't look like
that's the only one.

07:04.230 --> 07:05.700
It looks like this
one over here,

07:05.700 --> 07:10.856
this could also be
a candidate for c.
